it's been called an environmental  catastrophe of unprecedented scale  tons of dead fish washing up in the  river oda between germany and poland  poisoned by a so far mystery pollutant  which has contaminated the water  the most important thing is to find out  what is the cause and also  to remove as soon as possible  the dead fish and other organisms from  the river because  fish is  been eaten by  by birds by  other animals this is a really  a huge  task a huge challenge  volunteers have already collected more  than 10 tons of dead fish  so far mercury poisoning has been ruled  out  but tests are continuing after  unnaturally high salt levels were  detected  a mass die-off like this should be  flagged quickly  and polish officials have been  criticized for being too slow to tell  their german neighbours as the  contaminations spread  parts of the river are now so toxic  people have been told not to fish or  swim  the local fishermen are particularly  affected for them it is existential only  time will tell what effects this will  have over the next few years on the  other hand this is also catastrophic for  the tourism industry a good tourist  infrastructure has just developed here  on the oda  poland has offered a reward for catching  anyone found responsible for dumping  chemical waste into the river  but extensive damage has already been  done  with warnings the cleanup could take  years  siobhan robbins sky news
 

